<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malka (river)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Malka River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malka is a river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, about 216 km long, rising on the northern slopes of Mount Elbrus and flowing east and north-east through Kabardino-Balkaria to join the Terek near Prokhladny. Its valley and that of its tributary the Baksan formed the heartland of Kabarda, the domain of the Kabardian princes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malka-river.jpg" alt="Malka (river)" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Doran an Teg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malka_v_cherte_Prohladnogo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the sixteenth century Circassians occupied a broad territory from the Taman peninsula in the west to Kabarda on the Terek and <b>Malka</b> rivers in the east, and were known across the Black Sea world as warriors, horse breeders and, less happily, as a major source of slaves.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Kabarda formed in the 15th century when Circassian princes, traditionally descended from the semi-legendary Inal, settled the plains along the Terek, <b>Malka</b> and Baksan rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kabarda">Principality of Kabarda</a></li></ul></section>
